1
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:24,160
HOST: This is the 22nd of [May], 1985, in the library room of the Bethel Presbyterian Church, with Gertrude Tanner.

2
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:36,520
HOST: And she's going to give us a little bit of her early beginnings in Bethel, and the beginnings are also on the

3
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,840
HOST: side of the women's club.

4
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:52,440
GERTRUDE TANNER: You ladies have asked me to talk about looking back about the history of the club and of the area here, and

5
00:00:52,440 --> 00:01:07,460
GERTRUDE TANNER: I can start by telling you that old Fort Couch Road was an area here where Indians evidently came, and they had

6
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:14,460
GERTRUDE TANNER: to have a fort along that rushing stream that came alongside of those garages that are existing down there now.

7
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:25,900
GERTRUDE TANNER: They needed fresh water, so they had to build a fort by the stream.

8
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:26,500
GERTRUDE TANNER: And Mr. Couch, or Mr. Couch, must have given the land for the fort, because they named the fort Couch.

9
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:34,080
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now later, he had a son who was going to marry by the name of Nathan.

10
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:42,020
GERTRUDE TANNER: And he gave Nathan a piece of ground and maybe a whole farm for all I know, but at least he gave

11
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:46,900
GERTRUDE TANNER: him a place where he built a log cabin and later a stone house.

12
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:53,240
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now that stone house still exists and was part of the Martin farm.

13
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:56,940
GERTRUDE TANNER: So the Martins built a large addition, a frame addition, onto the stone place.

14
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:08,390
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now the Otts lived there on Kennebec Road, and that was probably a little lane that came up from the fort where

15
00:02:08,390 --> 00:02:12,070
GERTRUDE TANNER: his son and family were living.

16
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:22,230
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, this fort had to date back as far as 1750, I would judge.

17
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:31,010
GERTRUDE TANNER: I don't think it could be further back than that, but at least I would say that, because the church was organized,

18
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:36,490
GERTRUDE TANNER: the Bethel Church, over in the stone manse, what was the stone house there.

19
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:44,630
GERTRUDE TANNER: The church was organized in about 1776, is that about right?

20
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:59,490
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, people in those days had to travel by horseback or wagon to get to these different places, but they certainly must

21
00:02:59,490 --> 00:03:03,610
GERTRUDE TANNER: have gone to the Bethel Church over at this old stone land.

22
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:12,810
GERTRUDE TANNER: And later, a church was built on Bethel Hill, not far from where this fort had been, but probably was torn down

23
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:13,430
GERTRUDE TANNER: by this time.

24
00:03:13,430 --> 00:03:30,130
GERTRUDE TANNER: The reason that I know about Nathan Couch and his having lived there on the Martin Farm was because I had come

25
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:37,170
GERTRUDE TANNER: here as a bride in 1923 and had come out to see my husband's brother who was living on Brookside Boulevard.

26
00:03:43,500 --> 00:04:00,100
GERTRUDE TANNER: It seems that in the early days, around 1900, when they talked of putting a streetcar or an interurban from Castle Shannon

27
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:11,620
GERTRUDE TANNER: to Washington, PA, the Freehold Real Estate in Pittsburgh had a bright idea that they would buy up parcels of land, and

28
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:18,940
GERTRUDE TANNER: whole farms, in an area that was called, that they later named Brookside.

29
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,620
GERTRUDE TANNER: They named it Brookside Farm.

30
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,920
GERTRUDE TANNER: Well, naturally, with the fort having been right across the road, they named all the streets Indian names.

31
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:36,380
GERTRUDE TANNER: It was Kennebec, Arapahoe, Seneca, and so on.

32
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:46,140
GERTRUDE TANNER: But strangely enough, they took the main one of the rain roads that went from north to south and called it a

33
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:52,040
GERTRUDE TANNER: boulevard. It was anything but an elegant boulevard, but it was a road.

34
00:04:53,240 --> 00:05:06,140
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, my husband's brother built a house in the early development of Brookside, and if you came down Mohawk Road and you

35
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:11,060
GERTRUDE TANNER: didn't turn right or left on the boulevard, you would run right into this house.

36
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:20,580
GERTRUDE TANNER: I don't know who lives there today, but that was the house in the spring of 1923 that we got in our

37
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:24,500
GERTRUDE TANNER: Model T Ford and came out to call on Howard.

38
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:35,940
GERTRUDE TANNER: And when I saw Brookside in the spring, I knew that this was the place that I would love.

39
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:38,120
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I said, oh, if I could only live here, I'd be happy.

40
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:51,980
GERTRUDE TANNER: Well, strangely enough, on the other side of the interurban tracks and the stream, was part of this Martin Farm.

41
00:05:52,260 --> 00:06:08,560
GERTRUDE TANNER: And there on the side, on the lower side of Sylvan Road, were two houses with great big porches facing Brookside.

42
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:15,840
GERTRUDE TANNER: They were on the opposite side of the roadside, and I don't think they were called verandas in that space.

43
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:22,300
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now a veranda was more than a porch - a porch could be small, but these ran the whole width of the

44
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:22,900
GERTRUDE TANNER: house.

45
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:31,820
GERTRUDE TANNER: And you put rugs on them, and you put tables and an oil lamp or whatever, and you sat out there away

46
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:39,780
GERTRUDE TANNER: from the dust of the road, and you had this gorgeous view of Brookside in the spring, fall.

47
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,980
GERTRUDE TANNER: In the spring, they had not cut down trees like most builders do today.

48
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:56,620
GERTRUDE TANNER: They had even planted them. They had planted a white pear tree that was white in the spring, called a Kieffer pear.

49
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:04,100
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now the Kieffer pear, to eat it out of your hand, unless it was dead ripe, it was a grainy pear.

50
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:10,600
GERTRUDE TANNER: But if you cooked it, and I canned many a quart of Kieffer pears, they were just delicious.

51
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:13,160
GERTRUDE TANNER: I can't look at you or I'll say something.

52
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:20,060
GERTRUDE TANNER: This was what I was introduced to.

53
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:28,880
GERTRUDE TANNER: These two big houses that were facing Brookside had carriage houses.

54
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:33,300
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now they had come out here long before this interurban had been developed.

55
00:07:33,660 --> 00:07:42,640
GERTRUDE TANNER: They had come out here to the country as their summer home probably, and they had a place for the carriages and

56
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:44,100
GERTRUDE TANNER: a place for the horses underneath.

57
00:07:45,180 --> 00:07:46,980
GERTRUDE TANNER: And they, of course, later became garages.

58
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:54,840
GERTRUDE TANNER: One of them had the top torn off, where somebody - whether they stored grain there, I don't know.

59
00:07:55,140 --> 00:08:05,140
GERTRUDE TANNER: But the second house, the one that I was able to rent, was built right on the road, and you would wheel

60
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:13,420
GERTRUDE TANNER: the carriage in on the lower level, but it had a second level where they stored the oats for the horses and

61
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:15,100
GERTRUDE TANNER: probably had a place for a man to sleep.

62
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:26,920
GERTRUDE TANNER: But the horses were taken down the side of the road of the house, and the house underneath them, what would be

63
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:36,860
GERTRUDE TANNER: the cellar. Now that's what Charles and I rented on August 1st of 1923.

64
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:41,500
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now because we were young - I'd been born in Scranton, Pennsylvania.

65
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:51,380
GERTRUDE TANNER: Charles had been an officer in the First World War - but we had very little experience with houses.

66
00:08:51,740 --> 00:09:00,020
GERTRUDE TANNER: Little did we know that pigeons loved to follow horses, and where there's any grain, there are pigeons.

67
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:07,100
GERTRUDE TANNER: So that was our first job, was to clean out the rafters where the horses were kept.

68
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:16,160
GERTRUDE TANNER: Then we had to air the bedroom upstairs where the grain had been kept, because if you have oats, and if it's

69
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:19,840
GERTRUDE TANNER: damp weather, those oats can smell pretty terrible.

70
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:30,440
GERTRUDE TANNER: So my father came out in his Model T Ford to help me clean this carriage house.

71
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:41,220
GERTRUDE TANNER: We lived there two years when our first child was coming, and then we loved that hillside so much, and the view

72
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:47,530
GERTRUDE TANNER: of Brookside, that we bought a chunk out of the next farm, which was the Walden Farm.

73
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:57,730
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I have lived there 60 years, plus the two in the little carriage house, making 62 years.

74
00:09:58,130 --> 00:10:00,730
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I have never moved in a moving van.

75
00:10:01,170 --> 00:10:09,810
GERTRUDE TANNER: We had nothing when we came to the carriage house but a cedar chest and a trunk, which went on the back

76
00:10:09,810 --> 00:10:14,270
GERTRUDE TANNER: of the Ford. We carried everything else across the road.

77
00:10:14,390 --> 00:10:15,610
GERTRUDE TANNER: So I never moved.

78
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:27,210
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, the war was over - the First World War to end all wars was over - and building now started up

79
00:10:27,210 --> 00:10:37,530
GERTRUDE TANNER: again. Some houses had been built before the war, but without getting materials you couldn't build any.

80
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:40,290
GERTRUDE TANNER: And all the war effort was taking everybody's interest.

81
00:10:40,290 --> 00:10:49,570
GERTRUDE TANNER: So as soon as '22 and '23 came, or when I came to the area, the houses began to spring up.

82
00:10:49,570 --> 00:11:04,870
GERTRUDE TANNER: And after the Second World War, which was 20 years about later, we had been so established in the area, and because

83
00:11:04,870 --> 00:11:16,170
GERTRUDE TANNER: of the Depression, Charles had had to go into business for himself as an engineer, and we were interested in buying land.

84
00:11:16,990 --> 00:11:24,930
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, Charles was - he built dams, earth dams, for the state of Pennsylvania.

85
00:11:24,930 --> 00:11:35,310
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I love to tell people that one of the things that I feel is a lovely memorial for anybody's efforts all

86
00:11:35,310 --> 00:11:54,470
GERTRUDE TANNER: through their life was the dam and recreation park over there at down the line here, I'm kind of thinking right now,

87
00:11:54,570 --> 00:11:59,950
GERTRUDE TANNER: that big lake in the Beaver County, Raccoon Park.

88
00:12:00,170 --> 00:12:02,050
GERTRUDE TANNER: I couldn't say the word Raccoon Park.

89
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:12,710
GERTRUDE TANNER: Charles designed that, built it, and built the roads around as his construction company.

90
00:12:13,330 --> 00:12:18,530
GERTRUDE TANNER: Then he also built the one up at Shawnee Lake, which is outside of Bedford, Pennsylvania.

91
00:12:19,370 --> 00:12:25,690
GERTRUDE TANNER: And thousands upon thousands of people are there in the hot weather using those facilities.

92
00:12:26,850 --> 00:12:35,430
GERTRUDE TANNER: So my job was to drive the car around these country roads while he looked for the site for the dam.

93
00:12:35,430 --> 00:12:42,750
GERTRUDE TANNER: And those are things that I feel are a real memorial for our family to remember.

94
00:12:42,750 --> 00:12:52,270
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, because he was building these dams, he'd lose his men - they'd go to other jobs if he didn't keep them

95
00:12:52,270 --> 00:12:57,870
GERTRUDE TANNER: busy. And so we were able to buy what was left of the Martin farm.

96
00:12:57,870 --> 00:13:05,470
GERTRUDE TANNER: That was the house, and it was the house, the stone part, that Nathan Couch had built.

97
00:13:06,590 --> 00:13:18,490
GERTRUDE TANNER: And it included what would be a small area, a small farm, which was no longer farmed.

98
00:13:18,650 --> 00:13:25,050
GERTRUDE TANNER: The two daughters, Margaret and Almeda, were still living there when we bought it.

99
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:32,450
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, we didn't want that house - we already had a house of our own - but we had to sell it.

100
00:13:32,570 --> 00:13:39,790
GERTRUDE TANNER: People wanted to buy it. Here was a very old house with great big deep windowsills for the stone part of it.

101
00:13:41,010 --> 00:13:45,810
GERTRUDE TANNER: And they wanted to buy it, but they couldn't get a mortgage.

102
00:13:45,810 --> 00:13:51,230
GERTRUDE TANNER: Here was a house, the floor was right on the ground.

103
00:13:51,550 --> 00:14:04,590
GERTRUDE TANNER: Great big wide oak beams lay right on the dirt, and the walls were plastered with mud, horse hair, and lime.

104
00:14:05,390 --> 00:14:09,010
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now over those years, they had begun to disintegrate.

105
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:20,790
GERTRUDE TANNER: So the bank said, we do it, and we gut it, the whole thing down to the straight stones, re-plastered, put a

106
00:14:20,790 --> 00:14:25,770
GERTRUDE TANNER: foundation in it, and then built it up again.

107
00:14:26,430 --> 00:14:32,230
GERTRUDE TANNER: And that exists today, and the Otts own it and live in it.

108
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:39,410
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I like to think of those years when Nathan was there, maybe in a log cabin.

109
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:51,770
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, if Nathan were to come back and see what's going on today, he would be horrified, because Fort Couch Road is

110
00:14:51,770 --> 00:14:58,790
GERTRUDE TANNER: going to be - instead of a little trail that it once was, and then a dirt road - it's now going

111
00:14:58,790 --> 00:15:10,090
GERTRUDE TANNER: to be a four-lane highway. Three lanes, with a lane taking off to the various places that have to take off from

112
00:15:10,090 --> 00:15:13,250
GERTRUDE TANNER: a three-lane easily. So it's partly four and partly three-lane highway.

113
00:15:17,150 --> 00:15:30,830
GERTRUDE TANNER: I like to go back now a little to tell you ladies about the club, but I'll make that a little shorter

114
00:15:30,830 --> 00:15:33,010
GERTRUDE TANNER: because you can read the minutes.

115
00:15:33,090 --> 00:15:35,630
GERTRUDE TANNER: Here's the minutes book that I have right here.

116
00:15:35,630 --> 00:15:44,450
GERTRUDE TANNER: You can read the detail, but I will tell you briefly that how the club started

117
00:15:46,430 --> 00:15:55,730
GERTRUDE TANNER: way back in 1925, and it's exactly 60 years old today.

118
00:15:56,070 --> 00:16:07,710
GERTRUDE TANNER: We had, living in Brookside, first before the club was started there was a book club - I had to mention that

119
00:16:07,710 --> 00:16:20,650
GERTRUDE TANNER: - but people were conscious of getting together to socialize, so they were partly groups that got together for friendship as well

120
00:16:20,650 --> 00:16:22,830
GERTRUDE TANNER: as to read a book or have a book review.

121
00:16:23,030 --> 00:16:32,410
GERTRUDE TANNER: But Mrs. Riggs organized the first book club, and from that, three or four other book clubs took off when one would

122
00:16:32,410 --> 00:16:38,970
GERTRUDE TANNER: get too large. And I don't know if any exist today or not, but we would buy a book and have to

123
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:42,310
GERTRUDE TANNER: review it. That was good training for all of us.

124
00:16:42,490 --> 00:16:50,270
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, next year, in '25, was when Mrs.

125
00:16:50,470 --> 00:16:53,150
GERTRUDE TANNER: Edna Cully came to live in Brookside.

126
00:16:53,910 --> 00:16:58,070
GERTRUDE TANNER: And she is the one who thought we should have a women's club.

127
00:16:58,490 --> 00:17:06,550
GERTRUDE TANNER: She probably had come from an area where there was one, because she knew well how to organize it, and to write

128
00:17:06,750 --> 00:17:11,110
GERTRUDE TANNER: bylaws, and to get people interested.

129
00:17:11,450 --> 00:17:20,750
GERTRUDE TANNER: It was really organized to introduce people to each other - people that were coming in but wouldn't know their next-door neighbor

130
00:17:20,750 --> 00:17:27,310
GERTRUDE TANNER: well. And the club was to socialize as well as do a little civic work.

131
00:17:27,590 --> 00:17:28,870
GERTRUDE TANNER: That came later.

132
00:17:30,190 --> 00:17:36,330
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, I was the only charter member left, but that's why they asked me to talk today.

133
00:17:37,970 --> 00:17:48,070
GERTRUDE TANNER: And I can say that there was a time when this club was very flourishing.

134
00:17:48,230 --> 00:17:53,550
GERTRUDE TANNER: We had a drama department. We always had a garden department.

135
00:17:54,070 --> 00:18:00,330
GERTRUDE TANNER: Today's garden department is part crafts, but we do call it garden department.

136
00:18:00,850 --> 00:18:07,050
GERTRUDE TANNER: We had a musical choral group, and was quite active.

137
00:18:07,890 --> 00:18:15,930
GERTRUDE TANNER: In fact, the club was, well, we'll say maybe 25 years ago, a very active group.

138
00:18:16,690 --> 00:18:25,110
GERTRUDE TANNER: Now, the men had a civic organization even before the women were organized.

139
00:18:25,730 --> 00:18:33,570
GERTRUDE TANNER: They would meet at the Brookside trolley stop to go into work in the city and discuss the conditions.

140
00:18:34,150 --> 00:18:42,330
GERTRUDE TANNER: And probably through their efforts, we may have gotten streetlights and maybe the street signs were better put up.

141
00:18:43,050 --> 00:18:52,570
GERTRUDE TANNER: But it was the women's club - it still exists, and that is what we're celebrating today:

142
00:18:53,670 --> 00:18:58,310
GERTRUDE TANNER: 60 years of history right here in Brookside Farms.

